  "body": "Conjecture: the function open:creib.forge.conformance.oracle.recover_json_object, whose documented rule says 'The object scored is the last one inside a code fence when any fence holds one, else the last top-level object in the text', gives the same answer for two replies that the rule requires it to tell apart. Reply one is: ```javascript\\n{\\\"a\\\":1}\\n```\\n{\\\"b\\\":2}. Reply two is: {\\\"b\\\":2}. The rule requires recover_json_object to return {\\\"a\\\":1} for reply one, because the object {\\\"a\\\":1} is inside a code fence and is the last fenced object, and to return {\\\"b\\\":2} for reply two, because there is no fenced object and the last top-level object is {\\\"b\\\":2}. The check, however, returns {\\\"b\\\":2} for both, because its fence regex only matches ```json, ```JSON, or bare ``` fences, so the ```javascript fence in reply one is not recognized as a fence, and the object inside it is treated as a top-level object; the last top-level object is then {\\\"b\\\":2}. I would be wrong if the implementation actually recognized the ```javascript fence as a code fence and returned {\\\"a\\\":1} for reply one, or if the documented rule were read to exclude non-json fences, in which case the rule would also require {\\\"b\\\":2} for reply one and there would be no discrepancy."
